<!--
  =================================================================
  ISE LINKAGE EVALUATION PAGE TEMPLATE
  =================================================================

  WHAT THIS PAGE IS
  -----------------
  A linkage page evaluates a single CONNECTION between an argument (or
  evidence) X and a claim Y. The proposition under debate is not whether
  X is true and not whether Y is true. The proposition is whether X
  actually bears on Y the way the placement implies.

  Every linkage in the ISE argument graph deserves its own page when the
  linkage is contested or important. The linkage score is computed from
  this page's own argument tree, recursively, the same way belief scores
  are computed from sub-arguments. Audit-lock applies: no manual scoring.

  CANONICAL SOURCES (read these before editing this template)
  -----------------------------------------------------------
  Methodology page (the WHAT and WHY of linkage scores):
    https://myclob.pbworks.com/w/page/159338766/Linkage%20Scores
  Companion belief page template: src/pages/belief.astro
  Backing SQL schema:             sql/linkage_pages_schema.sql
  Architecture notes:             sql/README.md
  Argument scoring rules:
    https://myclob.pbworks.com/w/page/159333015/Argument%20scores%20from%20sub-argument%20scores
  Evidence tiers:
    https://myclob.pbworks.com/w/page/159353568/Evidence
  Belief Equivalency Engine (powers the rephrasings table):
    https://myclob.pbworks.com/w/page/21956921/Beliefs

  If the methodology page and the code disagree, the methodology page
  is canonical. File an issue rather than amending the methodology
  silently.

  HOW THIS RELATES TO THE BELIEF TEMPLATE
  ---------------------------------------
  belief.astro describes a CLAIM's place in the world: arguments,
  evidence, values, interests, costs, benefits, laws, compromises.
  This page describes a SINGLE EDGE in the argument graph: the bridge
  between one X and one Y. It deliberately drops the world-context
  sections because they describe what a belief means, not whether a
  logical connection holds.

  KEEPS from the belief template: argument trees (two columns, recursive
  scoring), hidden assumptions, bias risks, belief mapping.
  ADDS: proposition box, equivalent rephrasings with drift columns,
  five-step linkage check as a worked record, failure-mode worked
  examples (the Schiltz case is the canonical entry).

  WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS: THE SCHILTZ CASE
  --------------------------------------
  The evidence (96 court orders violated by ICE in January 2026) is
  high-tier T1. The parent claim (MAGA broke the norms that constrain
  the powerful) reads adjacent. The fluent restructuring looks fine on
  the page. It only fails the linkage check. Court order defiance is a
  separation-of-powers argument, not a wealthy-elites-evading-rules
  argument. Different mechanism, different parent. Fluent prose is not
  a linkage check.

  HARD RULES (every linkage page must follow)
  -------------------------------------------
    1. The proposition is "If X were true, would it necessarily
       [support / weaken] Y?" Not "is X true." Not "is Y true."
    2. Reasons to agree are about the BRIDGE: mechanism, entailment,
       scope fit, temporal fit, monotonicity.
    3. Reasons to disagree test known failure modes: True But
       Irrelevant, missing step, scope / scale / temporal mismatch,
       parent-mechanism mismatch.
    4. Equivalent rephrasings of both X and Y are mandatory. Holds
       under one phrasing = rhetorical. Holds under five = robust.
    5. Score cells stay blank until real sub-argument scoring exists.
       Audit-lock: no hand-assigned scores.
    6. Default sort is argument score. Vote ratio is SECONDARY.
    7. Contribution to parent = Argument Score (X) x Linkage Score.
    8. No broken links. No href="#". Plain text if the target page
       does not exist yet.
    9. Methodology changes go to the canonical Linkage Scores page
       FIRST, then this template, then individual pages.

  STRUCTURED DATA ARCHITECTURE (three layers)
  -------------------------------------------
  Layer 1: this HTML scaffolding.
  Layer 2: data-* attributes on every slot that varies between pages:
             data-ise-field="json.path"          canonical JSON path
             data-ise-section="name"             container tag
             data-ise-source="database|computed|generated"
             data-ise-audit-locked="true"        never hand-edit
             data-ise-link="json.path"           URL the renderer links to
             data-ise-pattern="mechanism|..."    argument row pattern
             data-ise-rephrasing-type="..."      rephrasing row type
  Layer 3: the JSON-LD <script> block below. Canonical machine-readable
           copy of every tagged field. If HTML and JSON disagree, the
           JSON wins. Field names match sql/linkage_pages_schema.sql.
-->
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>Linkage Template • Idea Stock Exchange</title>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" id="ise-linkage-data">
{
  "@context": "https://ideastockexchange.org/schemas/v1",
